  ////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
  // menumap-effects: the mood-context scratch overlay
  ////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
  // mood-context's field at 1840 is declared "(state uint32 32)", a generic
  // 128 byte scratch area each mood updater reinterprets as its own struct, so
  // (+ arg0 1840) resolves to (pointer uint32) and every lwc1 off it comes back
  // typed uint. update-mood-menumap then hits "FPR -> GPR applied to a uint in
  // (fpr->gpr f0-16) at 64" in update_from_stack_fpr_to_gpr
  // (IR2/FormExpressionAnalysis.cpp:965) at the mfc1 that passes the value to
  // seek!. This is NOT the rand-float-gen defect above: no mtc1 and no logior
  // are involved, the wrong type arrives through a mis-typed pointer.
  // Same fix and same shape as jakx's own update-mood-garage-brdroom /
  // update-mood-hiphog / update-mood-rustyh, and as jak3's whole
  // update-mood-<level> family. s5 is read at ops 40 to 75; the range runs to
  // 76 so it also covers the store that consumes the last derived address.
  // Measured: ending the range at 75 still clears the error but degrades the
  // loop body to (set! (-> (the-as (pointer uint32) s5-0) gp-1) (the-as uint
  // (seek ...))), while 76 gives the intended
  // (seek! (-> s5-0 current-values gp-1) (-> s5-0 target-values gp-1)
  //        (seconds-per-frame)),
  // which matches menumap-states' own (current-values float 5)
  // (target-values float 5).
  //
  // This entry is inert until the three menumap-effects externs in all-types.gc
  // are promoted out of comments; without them the object has no type analysis
  // at all. Verified under a scratch decode with
  // (define-extern update-mood-menumap
  //   (function mood-context float int none :behavior time-of-day-proc))
  // uncommented, which is the derived signature already sitting commented in
  // all-types.gc. Promoting it belongs to the menumap-effects landing.
  "update-mood-menumap": [[[40, 76], "s5", "menumap-states"]],
  // init-mood-menumap and set-menumap-target!: the same mood-context scratch
  // overlay as update-mood-menumap above, same object, same field at 1840.
  // Both bodies compute (+ base 1840) into a plain register with no loop
  // register (no s5 slot reserved), so the overlay pointer lives in v1 in
  // each function rather than s5.
  //
  // init-mood-menumap: v1 is set at op 0 (daddiu v1, a0, 1840) and last read
  // at op 12 (the target-values store address); range runs to 13 to also
  // cover the store that consumes that address, same as update-mood-menumap's
  // 75-to-76 pattern. Measured: ending at 12 still clears the "Cannot access
  // array of type int" error but leaves the target-values write as
  // (-> (+ (* a0-1 4) (the-as int v1-0)) 5), while 13 collapses both writes to
  // (-> v1-0 current-values a0-1) and (-> v1-0 target-values a0-1).
  "init-mood-menumap": [[[0, 13], "v1", "menumap-states"]],
  // set-menumap-target!: unlike the two functions above, the overlay add
  // reuses the SAME register for source and destination (daddiu v1, v1, 1840
  // at op 9, not a distinct dest like s5/gp or v1/a0), so a cast range
  // starting at op 9 itself forces the pre-add read of v1 to menumap-states
  // too and the add fails ("add failed: menumap-states <integer 1840>", a
  // structure plus a constant that is neither its exact size nor a 16-aligned
  // stride). The range has to start one op after the add, at 10, so op 9
  // computes its natural pointer type first and only subsequent reads of v1
  // get the override. Last read is at op 29 (the second branch's store
  // address, mirroring op 19's first branch); range runs to 30 to cover the
  // store that consumes that address, same shape as the other two entries.
  // Measured: starting at 9 throws the add-failed error above; ending the
  // range at 29 still clears it but only the first (zero?) branch collapses
  // to (-> (the-as menumap-states v1-3) target-values a0-2), because its
  // consuming store (op 20) is inside the range while the second branch's
  // consuming store (op 30) is not, so the second branch stays raw:
  // (-> (+ (* a0-2 4) (the-as int v1-3)) 5). 10 to 30 collapses both branches
  // to (-> v1-3 target-values a0-2).
  "set-menumap-target!": [[[10, 30], "v1", "menumap-states"]],
  // Debug-line scratchpad casts, the follow-up the cam-debug placement commit
  // flagged: cam-debug's remaining residue is almost entirely the raw
  // #x70000000 hardware/VU debug-line scratchpad address, built with
  // lui/ori and never typed, across camera-bounding-box-draw, camera-cross,
  // camera-line-rel(-len), camera-line-draw, camera-line-setup,
  // camera-plot-float-func, camera-sphere, camera-line2d, cam-debug-draw-tris
  // and cam-line-dma. jak3's ntsc_v1/type_casts.jsonc carries a full set of
  // per-op hints for this exact class, typing the constant as cam-dbg-scratch
  // (already live and correctly declared in jakx's own all-types.gc).
  // cam-layout was checked too (scoped decode, allowed_objects: [cam-debug,
  // cam-layout] against this worktree's config): it carries no #x70000000
  // reference anywhere in cam-layout_ir2.asm; its own residue is the
  // unrelated cam-layout-save-* stack-use-guessing class the cam-debug
  // commit already documented, so cam-layout is untouched here.
  //
  // Every function below was checked op by op against jak3's hint list using
  // this worktree's own cam-debug_ir2.asm (never jak3's numbering blind):
  // for each site, the raw disasm's "lui REG, 28672" load and the exact op
  // where that register is next consumed (a store, a load, or the add that
  // folds it into a computed pointer) were read directly, then matched
  // against jak3's op and register at that site.
  //
  // camera-bounding-box-draw, camera-line-draw, camera-line-setup,
  // camera-sphere, camera-line2d, cam-debug-draw-tris and cam-line-dma
  // transplant with zero drift: every op number and register jak3 declares
  // matches this worktree's own disasm exactly (camera-line2d and
  // cam-line-dma keep only the subset of jak3's hints that correspond to a
  // real site in jakx's shorter body; jak3's extra ops 6/14/20 for
  // camera-line2d have no counterpart here and were dropped rather than
  // carried over unused). cam-line-dma's own type is "vector", not
  // cam-dbg-scratch, matching jak3's choice for that function's t0 register.
  //
  // camera-cross drifts: the register sequence at every scratch site is
  // identical to jak3's (a0, a0, a2, a0, a2, a0, a1, ... 25 sites, same
  // order), proving it is the same code, but the op numbers grow apart
  // through the function (+0 for the first three sites, +4 through the
  // middle third, +8 through the next third, +12 for the last four),
  // consistent with a handful of extra instructions jakx has that jak3
  // does not. Re-derived from jakx's own disasm rather than transplanted.
  //
  // camera-line-rel-len and camera-line-rel each transplant three of four
  // and one of two sites verbatim; their last site drifts by a small,
  // non-constant amount (camera-line-rel-len's op 18 in jak3 is op 22 here;
  // camera-line-rel's op 8 in jak3 is op 12 here), same register both
  // sides, re-derived from the disasm rather than guessed from the offset
  // of the earlier sites.
  //
  // camera-plot-float-func transplants jak3's full 50-op hint list verbatim
  // (54 raw entries with four duplicates collapse to 50 unique ops, all
  // present in jakx at the same op and register) and needs one more site
  // jak3's own hint list does not carry at all: op 71, register a1, a
  // "daddu a1, v1, a1" folding the just-loaded scratch address into the
  // pointer used two ops earlier at op 68's sibling site. Verified directly
  // against the disasm; not inferred from the surrounding pattern.
  "camera-bounding-box-draw": [
